    (:feature "1000 clients over a LAN, a macOS server" :cells (- y - - - - - -) :evidence ((msgx "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof-macos.md and docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md at 813b4adbe3dec2eee496577e3da2c0d7d66b8063: 1000 clients for 600 seconds over the LAN, every reliability mode and size class passing the delivery checks, verdict pass in docs/QUALIFICATION-v1.0.0-preview-3.md")) :note "timing is tracked separately in the LAN throughput row above; the portable LAN cell is void (native-only ruling of 2026-10-24)")
    (:feature "1000 clients over a LAN, a Windows server" :cells (- - - - - n n -) :note "docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md records the Windows iocp and rio 1000-client LAN cells as not made for lack of an inbound firewall rule for the proof UDP port on the Windows server machine. The portable LAN cell is void (native-only ruling of 2026-10-24). No Windows 1000-client server LAN cell is qualified")))
  (:id "client-transports"
   :title "Native client transports (later)"
   :text ("The simplest native transport on the client on every OS: one connected UDP socket per client, batched calls, existing code. Owner, 2026-10-21: \"There is no reason for us to be portable on the client, portable is meant as a fallback there too.\" and \"I don't want anything hardcore like AF_XDP for example, but the simplest stuff... i'd like that.\" The native client ranks above portable, and portable ranks last, as the fallback, on every OS. The client transport deliverable has landed (PR #437).")
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Linux: the gso transport gets a client side (sendmmsg and recvmmsg)" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: landed as 5cc935ef and 8a5319cc; sdk/rocketnet_client_gso_linux.cpp, sdk/rocketnet_drivers.inc (client rank 10); test_client_gso_send_runs_segment_by_stride, test_client_gso_send_falls_back_on_live_refusal, test_client_gso_receive_splits_gro_by_stride, test_client_gso_receive_gro_fault_is_counted in tests/test_unit_client_gso.cpp and the gso client cells in ci.yml's transport family matrix, green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963")
    (:feature "macOS: the msgx client, ranked above portable" :cells (- y -) :evidence "macOS: sdk/rocketnet_drivers.inc ranks msgx at 10 above portable's 30; test_client_ranking_auto_picks_native_client, test_client_ranking_falls_back_to_portable, test_client_ranking_pin_is_honoured in tests/test_unit_client_transport_ranking.cpp and the msgx client cells in ci.yml's transport family matrix on macOS, green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963")
    (:feature "Windows: the iocp transport gets a client side (simpler than RIO)" :cells (- - y) :evidence "Windows: landed as c6648e90 and 0ccf5cb5; sdk/rocketnet_client_iocp_windows.cpp, sdk/rocketnet_drivers.inc (client rank 10), proofs/tla/RNClientIocp.tla; test_client_iocp_receive_completes_into_slots, test_client_iocp_wake_is_a_posted_completion, test_client_iocp_destroy_drains_posted_receives in tests/test_unit_client_iocp.cpp and the iocp client cells in ci.yml's transport family matrix on Windows, green in runs 40923772759 and 40925425656")
    (:feature "portable is the client fallback on every OS, ranked last" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 8a5319cc and 0ccf5cb5; sdk/rocketnet_drivers.inc; test_client_ranking_portable_is_last_everywhere, test_client_ranking_auto_picks_native_client, test_client_ranking_falls_back_to_portable, test_client_ranking_pin_is_honoured in tests/test_unit_client_transport_ranking.cpp and tests/test_driver.cpp, green on all three at 126ef062 and 81b6d3ef and in ci.yml runs 40925425656 and 40928229963" :note "Portable is available in production but native transports retain the production performance targets")))
  (:id "clients"
   :title "Further clients"
   :text "Clients beyond the desktop and server platforms; the client on Linux, macOS and Windows is in Connections and the connection layer."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Console clients (PS4, PS5, Switch, Xbox GDK)" :cells (n n n) :note "the platform source layers are under a different license, which is settled before the work starts")))
  (:id "browser-clients"
   :title "Browser clients (later)"
   :text "Browsers cannot open raw UDP, so a browser client reaches a RocketNet server through a WebTransport or WebRTC data channel front-end, either a relay or a second front-end in the server."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "WebTransport (HTTP/3 datagrams) client and front-end" :cells (n n n))
    (:feature "WebRTC data channel client and front-end" :cells (n n n))
    (:feature "Relay or second front-end that bridges either into the UDP transport" :cells (n n n))))
  (:id "dedicated-nic"
   :title "Dedicated-NIC transports (later)"
   :text ("These transports are only for a deployment where a second NIC is exclusively the game server's and the main NIC carries admin traffic; RocketNet never takes over a shared NIC, which is why AF_XDP (the kernel stack keeps the NIC, the server steers only its own packets) is the transport for shared NICs." "FreeBSD has no column of its own; its row is counted under Linux, the other server platform netmap serves.")
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "netmap transport on FreeBSD (netmap is in the FreeBSD kernel)" :cells (n - -))
    (:feature "netmap transport on Linux, only if netmap enters the mainline kernel (today it needs an out-of-tree module and a patched NIC kernel module)" :cells (n - -))
    (:feature "DPDK transport" :cells (n - n))))
  (:id "scaling"
   :title "Scaling and load"
   :milestone "next"
   :text "Many channels and many connections, measured."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Decided design rule, 2026-10-25: server CPU and memory cost is flat at its maximum from start, whatever the number of connected clients, so capacity is predictable and nothing surprises as players connect; a change that makes server cost scale with client count is a regression" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: docs/USAGE.md section Server cost is flat by design states the rule as a requirement")
    (:feature "1000-client in-process swarm with churn and garbage traffic" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; a2f59cf; rocketnet_test_swarm")
    (:feature "Channel count a build bound from 1 to 65536" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; ROCKETNET_MAX_CHANNELS with #error bound in sdk/rocketnet.h")
    (:feature "Nightly bounded soak" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: .github/workflows/nightly-soak.yml runs on the Linux, macOS and Windows fleet runners (846ae3d, 63a25be, 9ee42a2, 9e1b86f1, d6ffcfcc, 58ac8beb, 20f5c63d, c3409ab5); recorded per-transport soaks in docs/runs/linux-soak-30min-per-transport.md (adca4345), docs/runs/macos-soak-per-transport.md (31ad7862), docs/runs/windows-soak-native-server.md (3f900fa1) and docs/runs/windows-soak-portable.md (74b8bb7f)")
    (:feature "Message layer stressed at 1024 channels" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as a46ec919; tests/test_channels_stress.cpp and .github/workflows/nightly-channels-stress.yml run the 1024-channel stress on Linux, macOS and Windows")
    (:feature "Message layer stressed at 10k channels" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as a46ec919; tests/test_channels_stress.cpp and .github/workflows/nightly-channels-stress.yml run the 10,000-channel stress on Linux, macOS and Windows")
    (:feature "About 1000 real connections with channels on, at game-like load" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: bin/load (f49cc28, docs/LOAD.md) and bin/load_split (f8ac3731, docs/LOAD_SPLIT.md), tests/test_unit_light_profile.cpp (22d812c8, af5fa4e2, 336aa3fa, 3b3f7e9d) on Linux, macOS and Windows in ci.yml, the 1000-client cells in .github/workflows/nightly-soak.yml (d2c1ca6b, passing all five Linux cells in 485 s at c3409ab5), and docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md (1ca47733, 534cce2e, 813b4adb)")
    (:feature "Socket-level swarm with Go orchestration against real transports" :cells (n n n) :note "the in-process swarm, at the socket level")
    (:feature "Multi-day soaks past 2^32 packets on one connection" :cells (n n n) :note "soak_sim and soak run unbounded on real hardware for days of parallel clients before the connect layer is believed; polite and garbage traffic, counters asserted while running, past 2^32 packets on one connection so sequence wrap is routine. The nightly CI soak is the bounded sentinel")
    (:feature "Windows client overlapped send (WSASendTo + completion on the I/O thread) (later)" :cells (- - n) :note "dropped from v1.0.0 into the final (performance) milestone, owner, 2026-10-20: \"it is better for us to be stable today, and performant in the final milestone\". Evidence: the 0.94 s off-CPU sendto stall seen in 2 of 100 base suite runs (head ef8a2c9a) never reproduced in 50 traced runs (ETW). The overlapped prototype (head 28566f4856adb0b44072523b0a4c55bfd603c0e8) regressed off loopback (Windows client, Linux server, 6 runs): 69-77k packets sent with about 2,100 ring-full refusals against 614,400 at base, client update p99 from 0.01 ms to about 3 ms. On loopback the kernel delivers on the sender thread, so loopback send time is not the defect. ETW traces of the base and the prototype are kept with the evidence")
    (:feature "The load harness runs nightly on Linux, macOS and Windows (later)" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 846ae3d, 63a25be, 9ee42a2, d2c1ca6b, 9e1b86f1, d6ffcfcc, 58ac8beb and 20f5c63d; .github/workflows/nightly-soak.yml runs the load and per-transport soak jobs on the Linux, macOS and Windows fleet runners")
    (:feature "LAN throughput and 1000 clients, one server per transport: the design (later)" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a document; landed as f8ac3731 and 1ca47733; docs/LOAD_SPLIT.md and docs/LAN-PROOF.md")
    (:feature "LAN bench runner: the client load modes and the merge (later)" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as e13bdd67 and f8ac3731; tests/load/load_split.c, tools/load_split_verify.h, tools/check_split_load.py, tests/test_unit_load_split_verify.cpp, tests/test_unit_load_split_capture.cpp and tests/test_unit_load_split_barrier.cpp, green on Linux, macOS and Windows in ci.yml runs 40925425656 and 40928229963")
    (:feature "LAN bench runner: the server pinned-transport mode (later)" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as f8ac3731 and 013db4e3; tests/load/load_split.c server mode with ROCKETNET_SERVER_TRANSPORT and tests/test_unit_load_split_verify.cpp, green on Linux, macOS and Windows in ci.yml runs 40925425656 and 40928229963")
    (:feature "LAN bench runner: the orchestration (later)" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 1ca47733 and 5e0927a1; scripts/lan-proof, scripts/lan-proof-remote, scripts/lan-proof-selftest, docs/LAN-PROOF.md and docs/runs/lan-1000-proof.md")
    (:feature "Profile pass 1, measurement: soak across the matrix, the raw numbers committed (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")
    (:feature "Profile pass 1, report: where the time goes, and what to cut first (later)" :cells (n n n) :note "planned after v1.0.0-preview-1")))
  (:id "verification"
   :title "Verification"
   :text "How RocketNet is checked: unit tests without sockets or real time, functional tests, fuzzing, deterministic simulation, proofs and models. A proof or a model is platform-free: it is checked on Linux, where its job runs, and is — on macOS and Windows."
   :columns platforms
   :rows
   ((:feature "Unit suite with no sockets or real time" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; test_unit_registry.h; socket tests in tests/functional/; the rule enforced by tools/check_unit_purity.py and tools/check_unit_no_sockets.sh (f32c35de, c3ae8601: socket tests moved to the functional tier), both passing at 330e951e on Linux")
    (:feature "Functional suite over simulated lossy links" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; afa7ed8, 3d4f5b3; rocketnet_test_client_server_lossy_wire")
    (:feature "Seeded network simulator" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; a2f59cf; rocketnet_test_network_simulator")
    (:feature "The development library can simulate a bad link, through config alone" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 5182763; its cases run in the unit and functional suites ci.yml runs on all three on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164")
    (:feature "Every size, every type, every channel, both directions, every message verified: the loopback matrix" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as b0e8c84; its cases run in the unit and functional suites ci.yml runs on all three on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164")
    (:feature "Fuzz targets for packets, tokens and messages" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux: fuzz-smoke in ci.yml and fuzz-nightly.yml; macOS, Windows: ci.yml replays every target's corpus per push (af9c366); tests/fuzz/rocketnet_fuzz*.cpp; .github/workflows/fuzz-nightly.yml; every target smoked per commit ee35a0b; corpus replay on macOS and Windows af9c366; macOS: a real 900 s libFuzzer+ASan+UBSan run of all ten nightly macOS targets at a82dbf37 on the macos-bench Apple silicon host, 231,163,029 executions, 0 crashes, docs/runs/macos-fuzz.md")
    (:feature "Captured pcapng corpus replayed per commit and seeding the fuzzers" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; b4be701 pcapng_corpus_replay, pcapng_corpus_witness_unread_packet_goes_red; 0416321 pcapng_corpus_covers_control_packet_types; seeds 8cceab2; fuzz findings become pcapng regressions fcfe160 (tools/check_fuzz_regressions.py)")
    (:feature "Wycheproof vectors for ChaCha20-Poly1305, Ed25519 and X25519" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: ci.yml runs ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on the Linux, macOS and Windows legs on every push, green on all three in run 39271096164; f6fa899 tests/wycheproof; wycheproof_chacha20_poly1305_vectors 3fbe4dc, wycheproof_ed25519_vectors 410a517, wycheproof_x25519_vectors 2798d97, each with a witness that goes red")
    (:feature "Happy-path end to end: the real server and client binaries as separate processes" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: the happy-path job in ci.yml, one row per platform (1237a47); 1b95d8a tools/happy_path; the happy-path job in ci.yml")
    (:feature "Sanitizers: ASan, UBSan, TSan, MSan, LSan" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux: the asan + ubsan and tsan jobs of ci.yml (green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963), msan.yml (green in run 40937422503) and nightly-leaks.yml (079c3fa) on the Linux runners; macOS: .github/workflows/nightly-macos-sanitizers.yml (cd424514, 3b08c71f) runs ASan+UBSan, TSan and leaks across every suite and transport on macOS (docs/MACOS.md); Windows: .github/workflows/nightly.yml build-and-test-windows-asan and tools/windows/appverif_run.py (20e2a9a4, 5c79349a, 6f4ab487; docs/PLATFORMS.md)")
    (:feature "The MSan gate fails on crashed tests" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: landed as cafa290; MemorySanitizer runs on Linux only")
    (:feature "The staged-finish wire totals case and the MemorySanitizer check pass" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 8760e60, 6fa04f88, 29cfeb83 and 81b6d3ef; the wire totals cases run in ./bin/test and ./bin/functional on Linux, macOS and Windows on every push in ci.yml (green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963), and msan.yml runs on Linux (green in run 40937422503)")
    (:feature "Big-endian (s390x) runs" :cells (n - -) :note "9c82ef8 nightly-bigendian-system.yml boots a real s390x guest and runs the unmodified suite there; 9de809dc sets fail-fast: false so a failing ref no longer cancels the main leg, and the guest replays every case it can name alone three times. The io_uring codec fixture's byte-order defect is fixed (ea82dedd). A hand run of 9232be69 on a Linux x64 bench (TCG, 4 vCPU, 4G) printed UNIT_EXIT:0 (UNIT_SEC=370.70) and FUNC_PORT_EXIT:0 (FUNC_PORT_SEC=689.41), but FUNC_AUTO_EXIT:1 (FUNC_AUTO_SEC=841.47): test_server_port_zero_transport failed alone three times of three because the guest installs no clang with the BPF target, so XDP is compiled out (a supported build) and the xdp rung refuses with the compiled-out message, while the case asserted only the commit barrier. 8bbc8e41 makes the case require the compiled-out refusal when XDP is compiled out and the barrier when it is compiled in: green on x86_64 with XDP on and off, and on the s390x guest the case passes alone three of three on the auto rung (22.4 to 23.0 s); at af25b317 (that fix merged with main) on the same guest it passed alone three of three again (11.3 to 11.7 s). One full-system guest run of this head (all three suites) is still owed and is a known issue. test_posix_send_burst lost datagrams to receive-queue overflow at the guest's stock 256 KiB rmem_max; the guest now gets the fleet's 4 MiB ceiling and the case passed in the full portable rung. test_client_io_linux_handoff_round_trip failed once on a host near load 40 and passes alone three of three on both rungs. ci.yml's big endian s390x legs fail their toolchain check in runs 39271096164 and 39286548764. macOS and Windows have no big-endian target")
    (:feature "An instruction-count gate for the client frame and the server tick, on every push" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: landed as 56a9840; the gate runs Cachegrind, which runs on Linux")
    (:feature "A clang-format configuration derived from the netcode style, with a format check" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: landed as 612f270; the format is platform-free and checked on Linux" :note "the whole-tree reformat itself is a separate item")
    (:feature "The per-push workflows run on the self-hosted runners, held there by a lint" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: e529c71 landed the lint and the Linux jobs; 1237a47 moved the macOS rows and build-and-test-windows onto the self-hosted macOS and Windows runners")
    (:feature "The nightly workflows run on the self-hosted runners" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 4f24f4d, 96404a61, 20f5c63d and a8f24515; .github/workflows/nightly.yml, nightly-soak.yml, nightly-dst.yml, nightly-channels-stress.yml and fuzz-nightly.yml target the self-hosted Linux, macOS and Windows fleet runners, enforced by .github/scripts/check-runs-on.sh in ci.yml")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation testing: the design" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a document; landed as 1a2aab4")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation: the fault injector, as a TLA+ model first" :cells (y - -) :evidence "Linux: a model, checked by tlc.yml; landed as b522f5a")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 1: the seeded core, N clients and a server, one seed, exact replay" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 135a41c, 16f5387d and 553c79ba; tests/test_unit_dst_core.cpp runs in ./bin/test on Linux, macOS and Windows on every push in ci.yml (green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963) and in .github/workflows/nightly-dst.yml")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 3: the TLA+ properties as runtime checks" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as 1685d93, 16f5387d and 553c79ba; tests/dst_checks.h and tests/test_unit_dst_checks.cpp run in ./bin/test on Linux, macOS and Windows on every push in ci.yml (green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963) and in .github/workflows/nightly-dst.yml")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 2: the fault injectors (reorder, delay, clock skew, crash and restart), built from their model (later)" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as fd3d22d2 and 3d7adffd; tests/dst_harness.h, tests/test_unit_dst_core.cpp, tests/test_unit_sim_schedules.cpp, docs/DST.md and docs/audits/2026-10-24-dst-fault-schedules.md, running in ./bin/test on Linux, macOS and Windows on every push in ci.yml (green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963) and in .github/workflows/nightly-dst.yml")
    (:feature "Deterministic simulation, layer 4: per-push shards, the nightly campaign, and the planted-bug proof (later)" :cells (y y y) :evidence "Linux, macOS, Windows: landed as fd3d22d2, 3d7adffd, 16f5387d and 553c79ba; tests/test_unit_dst_core.cpp and tests/test_unit_sim_schedules.cpp run in ./bin/test on Linux, macOS and Windows on every push in ci.yml (green in runs 40925425656 and 40928229963), .github/workflows/nightly-dst.yml runs the named fault schedules and rolling seed sweeps on Linux, macOS and Windows, and docs/audits/2026-10-24-dst-fault-schedules.md records the fault-schedule audit")
